Q1. Deadlock Debugging (Code Trace)
 Problem Identification
 Process P1 locks R1 first.
 Process P2 locks R2 first.
 Both processes wait for 100 ms before requesting the second resource.
 P1 waits for R2, while P2 waits for R1.
 Both processes keep waiting forever, causing a deadlock.
 Root Cause
 Circular wait occurs:
 P1 → Holds R1 → Waits for R2
 P2 → Holds R2 → Waits for R1
 Mutual exclusion and hold-and-wait conditions are present.
 Optimization
 Acquire resources in the same order for both processes.
Example: 

 Lock R1 first.
 Then lock R2.
 This removes the circular wait condition.
Result
 Deadlock is eliminated.
 Both processes execute safely.
 System performance improves.
Q2. CPU Utilization Problem
 Problem Identification
 CPU utilization is only 30%.
 Processes repeatedly move between:
 Ready → Waiting → Ready
 Frequent waiting indicates excessive I/O operations.
 Root Cause
 CPU remains idle while processes wait for I/O.
 Poor scheduling causes unnecessary waiting.
 CPU is not efficiently utilized.
 Optimization
 Use asynchronous I/O to reduce waiting.
 Increase multiprogramming so another process runs while one waits.
 Improve CPU scheduling to keep the processor busy.
 Result
 CPU idle time decreases.
 More processes execute simultaneously.
 CPU utilization increases.
Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging
 Problem Identification
 Heap memory = 150 MB.
 Stack memory = 1 MB × 25 threads = 25 MB.
 Frequent page faults reduce performance.
 Memory accesses become slower.
 Root Cause
 Large memory usage increases page faults.
 Threads compete for memory.
 Poor memory locality causes frequent disk access.
 Optimization 1
 Reduce unnecessary memory allocation.
 Reuse allocated memory whenever possible.
 Optimization 2
 Reduce thread stack size or limit the number of threads.
 Improve memory locality to reduce page faults.
 Result
 Fewer page faults.
 Faster execution.
  Better memory performance.
Q4. Starvation Debugging
Problem Identification 
 Process P5 is in Q3 (Batch Queue).
Higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) continuously receive processes.
 P5 never gets CPU time.
 Root Cause
 Strict priority scheduling.
 Lower-priority processes are postponed indefinitely.
 This leads to starvation.
 Optimization
 Apply Aging.
 Gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
 Allocate CPU time fairly among all queues.
 Result
 Starvation is eliminated.
 Every process eventually gets CPU time.
 Fair scheduling is achieved.
Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
 Problem Identification
 System performs 20,000 context switches per second.
T ime quantum is only 1 ms.
Threads have an average burst time of 2 ms.
 Very frequent I/O requests increase switching.
Root Cause
 Small time quantum causes excessive preemption.
 High I/O frequency forces frequent switching.
C PU spends more time switching than executing.
Optimization 1
Increase the time quantum.
Threads complete more work before switching.
Optimization 2
Reduce unnecessary context switches using better scheduling. 
Batch I/O operations or use asynchronous I/O.
Result
Context-switch overhead decreases.
CPU efficiency improves.
Overall system performance increases.

